Add the clang target triple for 32-bit ARM Linux builds.

The engine's Linux block sets --target only for arm64, so an arm build gets
ARM cflags (-march/-mfloat-abi/-mfpu) while clang still targets the x86_64
host, and every compile fails with "unsupported option '-mfloat-abi='".
Mirror the arm64 branch. Hard-float to match the armhf sysroot and the
--arm-float-abi=hard the build is configured with.

The triple must say armv7, not arm: clang normalizes "arm-linux-gnueabihf"
to armv6kz-unknown-linux-gnueabihf, and the bundled toolchain ships its
runtime libraries (libc++.a, libunwind.a, libclang_rt.builtins.a) only under
armv7-unknown-linux-gnueabihf. With the wrong triple the engine compiles but
fails to link.

--- a/build/config/compiler/BUILD.gn
+++ b/build/config/compiler/BUILD.gn
@@ -399,6 +399,9 @@ config("compiler") {
       cflags += [ "--target=aarch64-linux-gnu" ]
       ldflags += [ "--target=aarch64-linux-gnu" ]
       cflags += [ "-DBORINGSSL_CLANG_SUPPORTS_DOT_ARCH" ]
+    } else if (current_cpu == "arm") {
+      cflags += [ "--target=armv7-linux-gnueabihf" ]
+      ldflags += [ "--target=armv7-linux-gnueabihf" ]
     }
   }

